WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 9.10 · Financial Management
Financial health and sustainability

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Judge financial health by resilience and dependency, not by the size of your first grant.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:26.652
Financial sustainability means being able to fund your mission over time, cope with shocks and invest in the future. You judge it by financial health, not by how fast your income grows.

4
00:00:27.052 --> 00:00:27.652
Four health measures

5
00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:38.981
Reserves and runway: Months of unrestricted reserves, and months you could run without new income. For example: Our bakery holds one month of reserves and aims for three within two years.

6
00:00:38.981 --> 00:00:50.681
Dependency: The share of your income that comes from your largest single source. For example: The youth fund is US$6,000 of US$48,000; no single café buyer exceeds 20% of sales.

7
00:00:50.681 --> 00:01:01.641
Flexible income: The share of unrestricted income you can use for core costs. For example: Stall and café sales give the bakery about 80% flexible income in year one.

8
00:01:01.641 --> 00:01:14.541
Full cost recovery: Whether your projects cover their fair share of shared costs. For example: Sales cover about 80% of full cost in year one; the target is 100% within 18 months.

9
00:01:14.941 --> 00:01:15.541
Why health beats size

10
00:01:15.541 --> 00:01:21.215
1. A big first grant or contract can hide a fragile model.

11
00:01:21.215 --> 00:01:26.519
2. Weak starting values are normal; the trend is what matters.

12
00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:33.193
3. They warn you before dependency or low reserves become a crisis.

13
00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:34.193
How you will use them

14
00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:39.867
1. In your annual review, to see whether your funding mix works.

15
00:01:39.867 --> 00:01:45.911
2. To choose the income mix that suits the form you are building.

16
00:01:45.911 --> 00:01:51.844
3. To set targets for reserves, dependency and flexible income.

17
00:01:52.244 --> 00:01:52.844
What to avoid

18
00:01:52.844 --> 00:01:57.407
Celebrating income growth while reserves and runway shrink.

19
00:01:57.407 --> 00:02:02.711
Tracking so many measures that none of them gets used.

20
00:02:02.711 --> 00:02:08.644
Setting no targets because your starting values look weak.

21
00:02:09.244 --> 00:02:11.044
You choose your health measures, starting values and targets.

22
00:02:11.044 --> 00:02:15.044
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:16.544 --> 00:02:19.644
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
